      <!-- DEEP DIVE EDUCATIONAL ACCORDIONS                                     -->
      <!-- ==================================================================== -->
      <section class="nn-section nn-deepdive-section">
        <header class="nn-panel-header">
          <div>
            <h2>Foundational Architectural Deep Dives</h2>
            <p class="nn-panel-desc">
              Rigorous mathematical formulations and systems engineering principles underpinning modern deep convolutional networks.
            </p>
          </div>
        </header>

        <div class="nn-accordions-group">
          <!-- Accordion 1: BatchNorm Bias Cancellation -->
          <article class="nn-accordion open" data-cnn-accordion>
            <button type="button" class="nn-accordion-trigger" data-cnn-accordion-trigger>
              <span class="nn-accordion-title">
                <span class="nn-acc-icon">⚖️</span>
                Why <code class="nn-code-inline">bias=False</code> Before <code class="nn-code-inline">nn.BatchNorm2d</code>? (Mathematical Cancellation Proof)
              </span>
              <span class="nn-accordion-chevron" aria-hidden="true">▾</span>
            </button>
            <div class="nn-accordion-content">
              <p>
                In virtually all state-of-the-art architectures (ResNet, ConvNeXt, EfficientNet), any <code>nn.Conv2d</code> immediately preceding an <code>nn.BatchNorm2d</code> sets <code>bias=False</code>.
                Here is the complete algebraic cancellation proof demonstrating why a convolutional bias is mathematically redundant.
              </p>

              <p>
                Let <Katex tex="x_i" /> denote the <Katex tex="i" />-th spatial activation patch in mini-batch <Katex tex="\mathcal{B} = \{x_1, \dots, x_m\}" />, <Katex tex="W" /> the convolutional filter weights, and <Katex tex="b" /> an arbitrary additive bias scalar:
              </p>
              <div class="nn-equation-box">
                <Katex tex="\hat{y}_i = W * x_i + b" display={true} />
              </div>

              <p>
                During training, Batch Normalization computes the mini-batch sample mean <Katex tex="\mu_{\mathcal{B}}" />:
              </p>
              <div class="nn-equation-box">
                <Katex
                  tex="\mu_{\mathcal{B}} = \frac{1}{m} \sum_{i=1}^{m} \hat{y}_i = \frac{1}{m} \sum_{i=1}^{m} (W * x_i + b) = \left( \frac{1}{m} \sum_{i=1}^{m} W * x_i \right) + b"
                  display={true}
                />
              </div>

              <p>
                The centering step of Batch Normalization subtracts this mini-batch mean <Katex tex="\mu_{\mathcal{B}}" /> from each activation <Katex tex="\hat{y}_i" />:
              </p>
              <div class="nn-equation-box">
                <Katex
                  tex="\hat{y}_i - \mu_{\mathcal{B}} = (W * x_i + b) - \left[ \left( \frac{1}{m} \sum_{i=1}^{m} W * x_i \right) + b \right] = W * x_i - \frac{1}{m} \sum_{i=1}^{m} W * x_i"
                  display={true}
                />
              </div>

              <p>
                Notice that the constant bias term <Katex tex="b" /> is subtracted out identically: <Katex tex="b - b = 0" />.
                Batch Normalization then applies its own learnable affine transformation parameters <Katex tex="\gamma" /> (scale) and <Katex tex="\beta" /> (shift):
              </p>
              <div class="nn-equation-box">
                <Katex
                  tex="y_i = \text{BN}_{\gamma, \beta}(\hat{y}_i) = \gamma \cdot \frac{\hat{y}_i - \mu_{\mathcal{B}}}{\sqrt{\sigma^2_{\mathcal{B}} + \epsilon}} + \beta"
                  display={true}
                />
              </div>

              <p class="nn-callout-text">
                <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> The learnable parameter <Katex tex="\beta" /> in BatchNorm already serves as the channel bias. Including <code>bias=True</code> in the convolution wastes GPU memory, increases parameter count, and calculates gradient updates for a parameter that is cancelled out at every step.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>

          <!-- Accordion 2: Memory Layout NCHW vs NHWC -->
          <article class="nn-accordion" data-cnn-accordion>
            <button type="button" class="nn-accordion-trigger" data-cnn-accordion-trigger>
              <span class="nn-accordion-title">
                <span class="nn-acc-icon">⚡</span>
                Memory Layout: NCHW vs NHWC (Channels Last) Acceleration on Tensor Cores
              </span>
              <span class="nn-accordion-chevron" aria-hidden="true">▾</span>
            </button>
            <div class="nn-accordion-content">
              <p>
                The physical memory layout of 4D tensors dramatically impacts cache hit rates, memory bandwidth utilization, and Tensor Core throughput on modern NVIDIA GPUs (Ampere, Ada Lovelace, Hopper, Blackwell).
              </p>

              <div class="nn-layout-comparison">
                <div class="nn-layout-col">
                  <h4>1. Default PyTorch: NCHW (<code class="nn-code-inline">torch.contiguous_format</code>)</h4>
                  <p>
                    Values along spatial width <Katex tex="W" /> are contiguous in memory. However, accessing all channels <Katex tex="C" /> for a given spatial location <Katex tex="(h, w)" /> requires strided memory loads with step size <Katex tex="H \times W" />.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    Because GEMM convolution algorithms (via <code>im2col</code> or Winograd) assemble channel vectors into matrix rows, NCHW necessitates expensive transpose/repack operations in VRAM.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <div class="nn-layout-col">
                  <h4>2. Optimized PyTorch: NHWC (<code class="nn-code-inline">torch.channels_last</code>)</h4>
                  <p>
                    All channels <Katex tex="C" /> at coordinate <Katex tex="(n, h, w)" /> reside in contiguous memory bytes.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    NVIDIA Tensor Cores perform matrix multiply-accumulate (<Katex tex="D = A \cdot B + C" />) on sub-matrices using 8-element or 16-element channel vectors directly. Under NHWC, 128-bit memory instructions (<code>LDG.128</code>) stream full channel vectors straight into Tensor Core registers without transposition overhead.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div class="nn-equation-box">
                <Katex
                  tex="\text{Speedup: } \text{Throughput}_{\text{NHWC}} \approx 1.20\times \text{ to } 2.40\times \text{ vs. } \text{NCHW on FP16/BF16 AMP}"
                  display={true}
                />
              </div>

              <p>
                Activating channels-last in PyTorch requires just two lines of code before training:
              </p>
              <pre class="nn-code-pre"><code class="nn-code-block">model = model.to(memory_format=torch.channels_last)
inputs = inputs.to(memory_format=torch.channels_last)</code></pre>
            </div>
          </article>

          <!-- Accordion 3: Depthwise Separable Convolutions -->
          <article class="nn-accordion" data-cnn-accordion>
            <button type="button" class="nn-accordion-trigger" data-cnn-accordion-trigger>
              <span class="nn-accordion-title">
                <span class="nn-acc-icon">📱</span>
                Depthwise Separable Convolutions (MobileNet Factorized Complexity)
              </span>
              <span class="nn-accordion-chevron" aria-hidden="true">▾</span>
            </button>
            <div class="nn-accordion-content">
              <p>
                Standard convolutions jointly map spatial relationships and cross-channel correlations in a single compute-heavy operation.
                Depthwise Separable Convolutions (pioneered in MobileNet and Xception) factorize this computation into two separate steps:
              </p>

              <ol class="nn-ordered-list">
                <li>
                  <strong>Depthwise Convolution:</strong> A spatial filter applied independently to each input channel (<Katex tex="G = C_{\text{in}}" />).
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Pointwise Convolution:</strong> A standard <Katex tex="1 \times 1" /> convolution that computes linear combinations across all channels.
                </li>
              </ol>

              <h4>Complexity Derivation:</h4>
              <p>
                For output dimensions <Katex tex="H_{\text{out}} \times W_{\text{out}}" />, kernel size <Katex tex="K \times K" />, and channels <Katex tex="C_{\text{in}} \to C_{\text{out}}" />:
              </p>
              <div class="nn-equation-box">
                <Katex
                  tex="\text{FLOPs}_{\text{standard}} = 2 \cdot H_{\text{out}} \cdot W_{\text{out}} \cdot C_{\text{in}} \cdot C_{\text{out}} \cdot K^2"
                  display={true}
                />
              </div>

              <p>In depthwise separable convolution:</p>
              <div class="nn-equation-box">
                <Katex
                  tex="\text{FLOPs}_{\text{depthwise}} = 2 \cdot H_{\text{out}} \cdot W_{\text{out}} \cdot C_{\text{in}} \cdot K^2"
                  display={true}
                />
                <Katex
                  tex="\text{FLOPs}_{\text{pointwise}} = 2 \cdot H_{\text{out}} \cdot W_{\text{out}} \cdot C_{\text{in}} \cdot C_{\text{out}} \cdot 1^2"
                  display={true}
                />
              </div>

              <p>The theoretical computational reduction ratio is:</p>
              <div class="nn-equation-box">
                <Katex
                  tex="\frac{\text{FLOPs}_{\text{separable}}}{\text{FLOPs}_{\text{standard}}} = \frac{C_{\text{in}} \cdot K^2 + C_{\text{in}} \cdot C_{\text{out}}}{C_{\text{in}} \cdot C_{\text{out}} \cdot K^2} = \frac{1}{C_{\text{out}}} + \frac{1}{K^2}"
                  display={true}
                />
              </div>

              <p class="nn-callout-text">
                For standard <Katex tex="3 \times 3" /> convolutions (<Katex tex="K=3" />) with <Katex tex="C_{\text{out}} \ge 64" />, this represents an <strong><Katex tex="\approx 8\times" /> to <Katex tex="9\times" /> reduction</strong> in compute and parameters with only marginal drops in top-1 classification accuracy!
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>

          <!-- Accordion 4: Dilated Convolutions & Gridding Artifacts -->
          <article class="nn-accordion" data-cnn-accordion>
            <button type="button" class="nn-accordion-trigger" data-cnn-accordion-trigger>
              <span class="nn-accordion-title">
                <span class="nn-acc-icon">🔍</span>
                Dilated Convolutions & Gridding Artifacts in Dense Prediction
              </span>
              <span class="nn-accordion-chevron" aria-hidden="true">▾</span>
            </button>
            <div class="nn-accordion-content">
              <p>
                Dilated (atrous) convolutions expand the effective receptive field exponentially without downsampling (pooling) or introducing extra parameters:
              </p>
              <div class="nn-equation-box">
                <Katex tex="K_{\text{eff}} = K + (K - 1)(d - 1) = d \cdot (K - 1) + 1" display={true} />
              </div>

              <p>
                For instance, with <Katex tex="K=3" /> and dilation <Katex tex="d=4" />, the kernel spans a <Katex tex="9 \times 9" /> spatial window while executing only 9 multiply-accumulate operations.
              </p>

              <h4>The Gridding (Checkerboard) Problem:</h4>
              <p>
                When multiple layers with identical dilation <Katex tex="d > 1" /> are stacked sequentially (e.g., <Katex tex="d=2, 4, 8" />), the kernel samples from a disjoint, checkerboard-like grid.
                Intermediate pixels in the receptive field are never sampled by any filter tap, resulting in high-frequency checkerboard artifacts and complete loss of local spatial consistency.
              </p>

              <h4>The Solution: Hybrid Dilated Convolution (HDC):</h4>
              <p>
                To eliminate gridding, dilation rates across consecutive layers must be relatively prime or follow a sawtooth sequence (e.g., <Katex tex="d = [1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 5]" />) such that the maximum distance between two sampled pixels satisfies:
              </p>
              <div class="nn-equation-box">
                <Katex
                  tex="M_i = \max(M_{i+1} - 2d_i, \; M_{i+1} - 2(M_{i+1} - d_i), \; d_i) \le K"
                  display={true}
                />
              </div>
              <p>
                This ensures dense, continuous sampling across the entire receptive field without holes or aliasing artifacts.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>
